# ============================================================
# TAKE-HOME NOTE — CSV vs Python Variables vs DataFrame
#
# A CSV file (e.g., admissions.csv) is just plain text stored on disk.
# To Python, it is “dead” until we load it.
#
# The file name lives on the file system:
#     data/raw/admissions.csv
#
# The variable name lives inside Python:
#     ADMISSIONS_FILE = RAW_DIR / "admissions.csv"
#
# Important distinctions:
# - admissions.csv  → real file on disk
# - ADMISSIONS_FILE → Python variable that points to the file
# - Capital letters apply to the variable, NOT the file name
#
# When we run:
#     admissions = pd.read_csv(ADMISSIONS_FILE)
#
# What happens:
# - Python reads the CSV text
# - pandas converts it into a DataFrame
# - The data becomes “alive” and usable
#
# Mental model:
# - CSV = storage format (dead text)
# - DataFrame = working format (living data)
# - ADMISSIONS_FILE = pointer, not the file itself
#
# Analogy:
# - Book on shelf        → admissions.csv
# - Bookmark label       → ADMISSIONS_FILE
# - Book opened to read  → admissions (DataFrame)
#
# Key takeaway:
# File names and variable names are different things.
# Capital letters name the pointer, not the file.
# CSV is stored; DataFrame is used.
# ============================================================
